          "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIn this section we list examples of fibre products and\nwe describe them.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nAs a really trivial first example we observe\nthat the category of sets has fibre products and hence every\nmorphism is representable. Namely, if <MATH_0001>\nand <MATH_0002> are maps of sets then we define\n<MATH_0003> as the subset of <MATH_0004> consisting\nof pairs <MATH_0005> such that <MATH_0006>. The morphisms\n<MATH_0007> and <MATH_0008> are\nthe projection maps <MATH_0009>, and <MATH_0010>.\nFinally, if <MATH_0011> and <MATH_0012>\nare morphisms such that <MATH_0013>\nthen the map <MATH_0014>, <MATH_0015>\nobviously ends up in <MATH_0016> as desired.\n\n<STRUCT_0004><STRUCT_0005>\nIn many categories whose objects are sets endowed with certain types of\nalgebraic structures the fibre product of the underlying sets also\nprovides the fibre product in the category. For example, suppose\nthat <MATH_0017>, <MATH_0018> and <MATH_0019> above are groups and that <MATH_0020>, <MATH_0021> are\nhomomorphisms of groups. Then the set-theoretic fibre product\n<MATH_0022> inherits the structure of a group, simply by\ndefining the product of two pairs by the formula\n<MATH_0023>. Here we list those categories\nfor which a similar reasoning works.\n<STRUCT_0006>\n<STRUCT_0007> The category <MATH_0024> of groups.\n<STRUCT_0008> The category <MATH_0025> of sets\nendowed with a left <MATH_0026>-action for some fixed group <MATH_0027>.\n<STRUCT_0009> The category of rings.\n<STRUCT_0010> The category of <MATH_0028>-modules given a ring <MATH_0029>.\n<STRUCT_0011>\n\n\n\n\n",
